[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c] === Summary content (3 topics) ===
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c] Topic: Merge Stitch UI components into unified QuickSetu Next.js app shell
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Trigger:   The user needed all separately exported Stitch UI screens for the QuickSetu grocery delivery app combined into a single, cohesive Next.js application with shared cart state and persistent navigation across every page.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Response:  - Added a global cart and navigation context providing cart items, active tab tracking, and cart drawer visibility, consumed throughout the app.
- Built a persistent bottom navigation dock with an integrated floating "View Bag" capsule that appears whenever items are in the cart, plus a slide-up checkout overlay mounted at the root layout so it is available on every route.
- Wired up all required routes: a voice-first home screen with a bento-style category grid, a vertical snap-scrolling product feed with weight selectors and quantity steppers, an order history page with a live delivery highlight, a live delivery tracking map with a status stepper, and a profile page with a language switcher.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Decisions: - **Shared state at the root layout**: Cart and navigation state were centralized in a single provider wrapped around the entire app rather than duplicated per page, so that the cart drawer, floating bag capsule, and bottom navigation all stay in sync across every screen without prop-drilling.
- **Static export compatibility retained**: The build was verified to still support static export mode, which constrains the architecture to avoid server-only Next.js features so the app can be packaged for mobile (Capacitor) as well as served as a static site.
- **Mobile-first shell with strict touch targets**: The layout was capped at a narrow max width and enforced minimum touch target sizing, prioritizing usability on phone-sized viewports over desktop layouts.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c] Topic: Design distraction-free single-product vertical reel screen
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Trigger:   The user wanted a modern, social-media-free product detail screen styled as a vertical snap-scroll "reel" for browsing a single grocery item, strictly avoiding likes, shares, or follower-style UI elements common in social apps.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Response:  - Created a dedicated product reel page with a full-height snap-scrolling layout split into a full-bleed photo canvas and a pure white product details card with rounded top corners and elevation.
- Implemented a fixed top bar with a circular back button, an "Express delivery" pill badge, and a search icon, all persisting during scroll.
- Added weight selector pills (500g/1kg/2kg/5kg) with emerald active styling, an add-to-cart button that transforms into a quantity stepper, and a floating cart summary pill above the bottom navigation.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Decisions: - **No social media affordances**: Like buttons, share icons, follower counts, and side-rail voice buttons were explicitly excluded to keep the screen focused purely on product browsing and purchasing rather than social engagement patterns.
- **Strict three-color palette**: The screen was locked to pure white, deep slate, and emerald green only, favoring a clean, minimal aesthetic over a broader color system to keep the product imagery as the visual focus.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c] Topic: Upgrade CI build pipeline to Node 20
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Trigger:   The Android APK build workflow needed updating to a newer Node.js version to fix a broken build.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Response:  The GitHub Actions workflow for building the Android APK was updated to use Node 20 instead of Node 18, and the dependency install step was changed to always run `npm install --legacy-peer-deps` instead of falling back from `npm ci`.
[2026-08-20T08:40:34.826Z] INFO  [QueueWorker] [trace=469461df628be57f13ce18555615047c]   Decisions: The install step was switched to always use legacy peer dependency resolution rather than relying on a clean lockfile install, prioritizing a working build over strict dependency version enforcement.
